**Theorem 4.5 (ordered epimorphism occurrence injection).** Fix, for each birth $b$, bijective enumerations of the source and target occurrences with birth $b$, indexed from zero and sorted by decreasing death, with infinity first. Repeated occurrences are separately indexed; choose their tie order once. An actual componentwise surjective natural morphism induces an injection from target occurrences to source occurrences that preserves birth and the within-class ordinal, and whose source death is at least the target death. Empty classes and essential occurrences are allowed.

**Proof.** Choose an earlier cutoff $r<b$ above every birth from either family strictly below $b$. For any $b<d$, choose a finite sample $t\ge b$ above every death strictly below $d$ and still below $d$. The epimorphism window $(r,b]$ at $t$ consists exactly of birth-$b$ occurrences with death at least $d$. Thus the target-to-source same-birth survivor-count bound holds at finite and essential $d$. A target occurrence of ordinal $k$ has $k+1$ earlier-or-equal occurrences with death at least its own. Match it to source ordinal $k$; a shorter source death would force all eligible source occurrences into the first $k$ positions, contradicting the bound.

For both constructions the enumeration is a parameter, not an assumed matching or count inequality. Sorted enumerations are supplied by the existing finite-order sorting library, using a fixed occurrence tie order. Within monomorphism classes and within epimorphism classes, the injections compose when the intermediate enumeration is shared: ordinal preservation is transitive, and a class enumeration is injective, so the composite agrees occurrence by occurrence with the injection of the actual composite morphism. Sorting and composition do not establish same-image quantitative estimates or the exact stability endpoint.

